Headwind is the wind component along the runway. Crosswind in X10 is `=ABS(X8*SIN(DEGREES(U7-X7)))`, which uses DEGREES in the same way. The matching headwind formula uses COS in place of SIN.

The inputs are runway U7, wind direction X7 and wind speed X8. I copied the existing DEGREES pattern for consistency, so the result is `=X8*COS(DEGREES(U7-X7))`. Headwind can be negative, which would indicate a tailwind, so I left out ABS.

{"formula": "=X8*COS(DEGREES(U7-X7))"}

The column header is "% Away from Ideal Allocation", and U15 sums U8:U14. The natural calculation is the difference between the new portfolio percentage (S8) and the desired percentage (T8). Since the sum would cancel out if signed, an absolute value is plausible, but the simple difference is the most likely intent.

I chose the absolute difference because U15 sums the column, and an absolute value keeps the total meaningful.

{"formula": "=ABS(S8-T8)"}

Column P is headed "Average" and sits next to "Last Amount" in column O. The most likely intent is the average of the non-zero amounts in the row.

AVERAGEIF ignores zeros when given the criterion "<>0", so `=AVERAGEIF(B2:M2,"<>0")` fits. This matches the range and criterion style already used in O2.

{"formula": "=AVERAGEIF(B2:M2,\"<>0\")"}

The formula below is an estimate. Column F is labelled "Average", and column E holds 6-second time buckets. Column G has the manual averages of the Close values in column B for each bucket. That suggests F should average column B for the rows whose time in column A falls in the 6 seconds before the time in E5.

The manual averages in column G are slightly inconsistent in places, so this will not match them exactly.

{"formula": "=AVERAGEIFS($B$5:$B$370,$A$5:$A$370,\">=\"&(E5-\"0:6.0\"),$A$5:$A$370,\"<\"&E5)"}

Cell M37 sits under the "SAP Total" header, in the same row as L37 (Planned Total = 500), G37 (Overhead) and I37 (Interest). The most likely intent is a total of planned cost plus overhead and interest for that row.

Evidence for this: M35 is labelled "GRAND TOTAL MATERIALS + AFUDC + OH", and the materials rows follow the same pattern, so the row total should be L37+G37+I37. Rounding up with CEILING, as M8 does, is a possible alternative, but it is less certain than the plain sum.

{"formula": "=L37+G37+I37"}

H3 is total loss to date in stones and pounds. G3 holds the total loss in lbs, so the stones are INT(G3/14) and the remaining pounds are MOD(G3,14). A text result such as "0st 2.5lbs" fits the "ST & LBS" header.

A plain numeric formula can't hold both units in one value, so I'll return text built from those two parts.

{"formula": "=INT(G3/14)&\"st \"&MOD(G3,14)&\"lbs\""}

The Projected Census column should count the patients present on the date in each row. Using the admission date in column B as that row's date, the formula counts rows where the admission date is on or before that date and the discharge date is on or after it.

I'm assuming the date is B3, since there is no separate date column. The ranges are anchored to match the existing G1 formula.

{"formula": "=COUNTIFS($B$3:$B$912,\"<=\"&B3,$C$3:$C$912,\">=\"&B3)"}

E39 is the Total cell for the fecal coliform column. Its neighbors use `=SUM(x8:x38)`, but E8:E38 holds only text values such as "<1", so SUM would return 0. The Total row has no clear convention for this column, so the closest pattern is the one used by adjacent columns.

The column is #/100mL, which in permit reports is normally summarized as a geometric mean, but the prefix gives no sign of that intent. I'll follow the row pattern.

{"formula": "=SUM(E8:E38)"}

The cell C7 sits under the "Years of Service" header and refers to the join date in B7. The leave-accrual columns start at 2025-01-01 (E6), so years of service is most likely measured as of that date. D7 uses LOOKUP(C7,{1,10},...), so C7 must be a whole number of years, which suggests DATEDIF.

The user could have used TODAY(), but the accrual schedule is anchored to 2025-01-01, so E6 is the better reference date. Anchoring it as `$E$6` lets the formula be copied down to the other rows.

{"formula": "=DATEDIF(B7,$E$6,\"y\")"}

B16 is blank while B15 holds the DSUM for "Base Salary". The columns C through E follow a pattern of a cumulative DSUM minus the sum of the rows above, so B16 should follow the same pattern.

The pattern in C16 is `=DSUM('Employee Data'!$A$1:$P$405,"Bonus",$K$14:L16)-SUM($C$15:C15)`. For B16, the equivalent is `=DSUM('Employee Data'!$A$1:$P$405,"Base Salary",$K$14:L16)-SUM($B$15:B15)`.

{"formula": "=DSUM('Employee Data'!$A$1:$P$405,\"Base Salary\",$K$14:L16)-SUM($B$15:B15)"}

End date = start date plus tenure in years, usually minus one day. The common approach is EDATE(B3,C3*12)-1.

Check with the first row: 2018-02-15 plus 20 years gives 2038-02-15, so the end date is 2038-02-14.

{"formula": "=EDATE(B3,C3*12)-1"}

Row 14 is "Discount Rate", and the PV formulas use continuous compounding, EXP(-C10*I13). The discount factor for I14 would therefore be EXP(-$C$10*I13).

The existing formulas in row 16 use relative references to C10, D10 and E10, which is a mistake. The more sensible choice for I14 is the discount factor with an absolute reference to the yield.

{"formula": "=EXP(-$C$10*I13)"}

Column I is "RELEASE", and the neighbouring columns are G "Expected AMOUNT" and H "KEEP". The most likely intent is that the release amount is the expected amount minus the amount kept.

Row 2: 371 - 86 = 285.

{"formula": "=G2-H2"}

The column is titled "Time Rounded" and the timestamps are roughly hourly, so I guessed it rounds the time in C2 to the nearest hour. This is a guess from the header and data, not from anything the sheet states directly.

{"formula": "=ROUND(C2*24,0)/24"}

A time like 23:58 rounds up to 24:00, which is a full day (1), so E would label it "DAY".

The row label is "Inflation adjusted", so B9 should discount the future value (B8) by inflation over the number of years (B6).

Inflation is in B5, so the formula is B8/(1+B5)^B6.

{"formula": "=B8/(1+B5)^B6"}

I can't see Punnam's actual formula, since L8 and the rest of column L are empty. This is my best guess, based on the pattern in the neighbouring columns.

Column L sits under the "Punnam" label, between Bo_Ry's `=MOD(F8-E8,1)*24` and AliGW's HOUR-based formulas. The data treats an identical start and end (12:00 AM to 12:00 AM) as 24 hours. It also treats an end time earlier than the start (4:00 PM to 12:00 AM) as running past midnight. This formula covers both cases: when the end is at or before the start, it adds one day, then converts to hours.

{"formula": "=IF(F8<=E8,1+F8-E8,F8-E8)*24"}

Time Billed is likely time worked rounded up to a billing increment, such as 15 minutes. A common formula for this is =CEILING(G3,"0:15"). The prefix is only "=", so I'm assuming that intent.

The sheet gives no stated increment, but the Time Billed column sits right after Time Worked, so rounding G3 up to a billing increment is the most plausible pattern. I'm also assuming blank rows should stay blank, so I'm wrapping the formula in a check for an empty date or time. Because G3 is a time value, I'm using CEILING with "0:15".

{"formula": "=IF(G3=\"\",\"\",CEILING(G3,\"0:15\"))"}

This is a best guess, since the prefix is only `=`. L2 is "BOTH" and L3 is "2ND SCORE", so L4 looks like the next-best score after the top WAGC score in J4 and the top CMT score in K4. In the rows I checked (4, 5, 6 and 8), that is the 3rd-largest score in B4:I4. I wrapped it in IFERROR because shooters with fewer than three scores would otherwise return #NUM!, which would break the SUM in M4.

The formula can be wrong when the top three scores are all from the same series, because J4 and K4 would then not both be among them.

{"formula": "=IFERROR(LARGE(B4:I4,3),0)"}

The label in D1 is "Date Min", so E1 should hold the earliest month start. G1 (Date Max) uses EOMONTH(MAX(B3:C42),0)+1, which is the first day of the month after the latest date. The matching minimum is the first day of the month containing the earliest date. That is EOMONTH(MIN(B3:C42),-1)+1.

The formula covers B3:C42 to mirror G1. Column A has earlier dates, but G1 ignores it, so I followed G1's range.

{"formula": "=EOMONTH(MIN(B3:C42),-1)+1"}

{"formula": "=MIN(B2:S2)"}

Wait — column U is "Highest", so it should use MAX, not MIN. Ranks here are numbers where 1 is best, but "Highest" most likely means the largest value in the row.

{"formula": "=MAX(B2:S2)"}

The D column formulas reference D$18 for the number of months (e.g. `EDATE(D$20,-D$18+1)`), but D18 is empty. The YTD block's period count should be the number of months from the start of the fiscal year (July) through the end date in D20. With a fiscal year starting in July, the month count is MOD(MONTH(D20)-7,12)+1. With D20 = Dec 2022, that gives 6, which is the YTD period.

Alternatively, the formula could use B4 as the start: =DATEDIF(B4,D20,"m")+1, which also gives 6. The fiscal-year version is more general, but the DATEDIF version is simpler and ties to the start date in the data. The YTD label suggests the fiscal-year logic, so I'll use the MOD version based on the start of the fiscal year in B4.

{"formula": "=MOD(MONTH(D20)-MONTH($B$4),12)+1"}

Row 29 is the biweekly total row, and E29 is labeled "Biweekly Total Hrs." The neighbouring cells M29, O29 and Q29 follow the pattern SUM(x16,x27). Column G holds the total hours (G16 and G27), so G29 most likely follows the same pattern.

M29 and the others wrap the sum in an IF, because their source cells can be blank. G16 and G27 are always numeric, so a plain sum is enough.

{"formula": "=SUM(G16,G27)"}

Column E is labeled "Checksum", and the row layout suggests a check on each row. The most likely intent is a per-row calculation such as the duration between the start and end dates, with E11 holding the total.

The simplest formula for E3 that fits this is the number of days between the two dates:

{"formula": "=D3-C3"}

Cell B10 is labeled "z (Z-score)", and B9 holds alpha/2. The z-score for a two-sided confidence level is NORM.S.INV(1-alpha/2). Since B9 = (1-B4)/2 = 0.025, z = NORM.S.INV(1-B9).

An alternative is NORM.S.INV(B4+B9), which gives the same value (0.975). I'll use 1-B9.

{"formula": "=NORM.S.INV(1-B9)"}

The block at E:F has the same layout as B:C, where C4 is `=NPV(C3/12,C8:C33)-C7`. The block at H:I uses `=NPV(I3/12,I7:I33)`, which treats Feb 11 as the start of period 1. The header over H says "With Feb 11 being end of period 1".

F4 most likely follows the pattern of the first block, adjusted for F. The E:F block has no header, so it could be either variant. The first block's formula is the most probable choice.

{"formula": "=NPV(F3/12,F8:F33)-F7"}

F3 sits next to the label "Pearson II" in E3, and F2 already holds `=PEARSON(C2:C17,A2:A17)^2`. The most likely intent is a second R² calculation using the same data, such as RSQ over the same ranges.

Another possibility is that "Pearson II" correlates the actual Y values with the estimated Y values, for example `=PEARSON(B2:B17,D2:D17)^2`. That would measure the fit on the original scale, whereas F2 measures it on the ln scale. Because column D holds the estimated Y values, this reading seems the more probable one.

{"formula": "=PEARSON(B2:B17,D2:D17)^2"}

Column F is "Principle" and is empty. The balance formula in G17 is =D6+F17, so principal is negative, which matches PMT being negative. Principal paid = payment minus interest: D17-E17, where PMT and IPMT are both negative, so the result is negative and G17 = D6 + F17 reduces the balance correctly.

An alternative would be PPMT, but the pattern of the sheet (D and E columns present) suggests D17-E17.

{"formula": "=D17-E17"}

The cell is labeled "Bond Price", and the inputs match the arguments of Excel's PRICE function: settlement, maturity, rate, yld, redemption, frequency, basis.

PRICE returns the price per $100 face value. The redemption value here is 1000, so the intended formula is probably PRICE(B1,B2,B3,B4,B5,B6,B7). Since B5 is 1000, that would give a price per 1000 only if the redemption were 100. A more careful version would scale the result by B5/100, but the simplest and most likely intent is the plain PRICE call.

{"formula": "=PRICE(B1,B2,B3,B4,B5,B6,B7)"}

The neighbouring cells M4 and O4 (and column L) follow a consistent pattern, and N3 is the "not Maori" analogue of M3. N4 is the lower-quartile cell for the Pākehā & Others column, so it should mirror M4 with the condition changed to `<>"Maori"`.

M4 is `=QUARTILE(IF(W$17:W$500="Maori",S$17:S$500),1)`, so N4 becomes:

{"formula": "=QUARTILE(IF(W$17:W$500<>\"Maori\",S$17:S$500),1)"}

Column I is "Average", with H as total pins and F as total games. In row 5, H5 = CELL("contents",G5), which is the total pins so far, and F5 = CELL("contents",E5), the total games so far. The average is therefore total pins divided by total games.

The prefix is only "=", so the formula is the plain division, and I'll guard against a zero denominator.

{"formula": "=H5/F5"}

K22 is "Total Days per Trip". The rows above it give the pieces: K19 is travelling days per ship (round trip plus Suez), K20 is days loading and K21 is days unloading.

The total is therefore the sum of those three cells. The model's existing style uses `=+`, but the prefix is only `=`, so I'll complete it with `SUM`.

{"formula": "=SUM(K19:K21)"}

The J column, "Logged Time", should hold just the time portion of the date-time in column B. The other formulas compare J to A, where A is a time-only value, so J has to be time-only as well.

The formula below subtracts the date part from the date-time and leaves only the time:

{"formula": "=B2-INT(B2)"}

J2 should hold the earliest date attended, taken from the date helper columns C, E, G and I. MIN ignores the empty-string text results, so it works across them.

Because the helper columns are not contiguous, I list each one separately. The result will show as a serial number unless the cell is formatted as a date.

{"formula": "=MIN(C2,E2,G2,I2)"}

I can't tell your intent for sure, so this is a best guess. It copies the lookup pattern in G5 but pulls from the payroll deduction table (columns O to U) instead of the premium table.

For plan 1, 2 or 3 it takes the Employee column for that plan (O, Q or S). It then matches the age in C5 against the age column U.

{"formula": "=IF(ISNUMBER(D5),INDEX(CHOOSE(D5,$O$8:$O$73,$Q$8:$Q$73,$S$8:$S$73),MATCH(C5,$U$8:$U$73,0)),0)"}

The user is in M5, under the "Decision Date" header, next to the Person ID lookup value in L5 (AAA4). The intended formula is a lookup of the decision date for that person ID.

The data sits in C3:C9 (Person ID) and J3:J9 (Decision Date), so an XLOOKUP fits.

{"formula": "=XLOOKUP(L5,C3:C9,J3:J9)"}

The column H is "% of Business", and the phase lookup table is at A8:B11. The old column I already holds `=VLOOKUP(G2,$A$8:$B$11,2,FALSE)`, so H2 most likely uses the same lookup, perhaps with a newer function such as XLOOKUP.

The prefix is only `=`, so I'll complete it with the most probable lookup formula for G2.

{"formula": "=VLOOKUP(G2,$A$8:$B$11,2,FALSE)"}

Magnesium sulphate is dosed at 40 mg/kg over 20 minutes, so the formula multiplies the weight in C6 by 40. Neighbouring dose formulas use the same pattern of a calculation joined to a unit string, for example `=0.2*C6&" ml"` in I18.

The calculation could also carry a maximum-dose check, but the sheet gives no maximum for this drug, so I kept it to the simple form.

{"formula": "=C6*40&\" mg\""}

Column D is empty beside the State, Status and Product columns, and H2:I4 holds a lookup table that maps state names to abbreviations. The likely intent is to look up the state abbreviation for A2.

Since the prefix is only `=`, I'm predicting a lookup formula with XLOOKUP.

{"formula": "=XLOOKUP(A2,$H$2:$H$4,$I$2:$I$4,\"Not Found\")"}

I'm inferring this formula from the layout, so treat it as a best guess. J2 sits next to the description "Low_No. of pulses_15 Days", and K2 holds the expected check value of 2.

Column F is a helper that restarts at 1 whenever the flow category in column C changes. Counting rows where C is 1 (low) and F is 1 (the first day of a run) therefore counts the number of low-flow pulses. The first 15 days are rows 18 to 32 (A1 to A15). In that range, low-flow runs start at rows 23 and 32, which gives 2 and matches K2.

{"formula": "=COUNTIFS(C18:C32,1,F18:F32,1)"}

The cell F34 sits beside C34, which holds `=LARGE($I$7:$I$28,1)` for the "1st" row of the Week 1 top 3 overage block. The neighbouring lookup cells, such as F43 and F50, follow the same pattern, returning the employee name for the value in column C.

F50 uses `=VLOOKUP(C50, CHOOSE({1,2},I7:I28,A7:A28),2,FALSE)`. The matching formula for F34 should therefore be:

{"formula": "=VLOOKUP(C34, CHOOSE({1,2},$I$7:$I$28,$A$7:$A$28),2,FALSE)"}

Start date for the next task is likely the previous start date plus the duration in days (or weeks). B7 is a start date; E7 is the time in days considering work load. Probably the formula is =B7+E7, which gives the next task's start.

Since E is in working days (weeks*5), WORKDAY would be a better fit: =WORKDAY(B7,E7). That is the likely intent, but the plain addition is the simplest. Using WORKDAY handles the working-day logic that E (days = weeks*5) implies.

{"formula": "=WORKDAY(B7,E7)"}

The column header is "N-MSD", which most likely means the standard deviation over the same N-day window used for the moving average in H. N is stored in P2 (4), but H2 uses a fixed window of 4 rows, so I'll match that window.

Whether to use STDEV.P or STDEV.S is a judgement call. Bollinger bands conventionally use the population standard deviation, so I chose STDEV.P.

{"formula": "=STDEV.P(E2:E5)"}

The label in B2 is "std", so C2 most likely holds a standard deviation of A2:A6. The neighbouring formulas use the range A2:A6, and E2 (=C2*2) points the same way, since it looks like a multiple of the standard deviation used for an upper boundary.

Both STDEV and STDEV.S would work here. I chose STDEV.S because it is the current sample standard deviation function.

{"formula": "=STDEV.S(A2:A6)"}

I'm inferring the intent from the sheet, so this is a best guess. I3 (86.668) equals the average of row A (118.847, 66.657 and 74.5). H1 asks for the output "without referencing spill", so I used the fixed range D3:F7 instead of `D3#`. The formula returns the average of each row, spilling down from H3.

I couldn't work out how J3 (64.4945) was calculated, so I ignored it.

{"formula": "=BYROW(D3:F7,LAMBDA(r,AVERAGE(r)))"}

Row 7 is labeled "Standard Deviation", and the neighboring rows follow the pattern `=FUNC(BATE!$C$2:$C$807)`. For B7 the sheet is BATE, so the standard deviation formula follows the same pattern.

The choice between STDEV and STDEV.S is a guess. The existing statistics use older function names such as QUARTILE, which points to STDEV. Either would give the same result.

{"formula": "=STDEV(BATE!$C$2:$C$807)"}

Cell H38 sits in the "Majority?" row, directly under Total Cast (H35). A first-round candidate has a majority when their votes exceed half of the total cast, so the formula checks whether the highest count in H9:H34 is more than half of H35.

I chose to return Yes or No rather than naming the winner, since the row label "Majority?" reads as a yes/no question.

{"formula": "=IF(MAX(H9:H34)>H35/2,\"Yes\",\"No\")"}

BI4 is headed "STDEVA", and the cells below it (BI6 onward) are empty. Cell BJ6 holds `=BI6/BH6`, which is Desv/Media (standard deviation divided by mean). BH5 already holds `=AVERAGE(C5:BE5)`, so BI5 most likely follows the same pattern with a standard deviation over the same range.

The header says STDEVA, so the formula is built on that function.

{"formula": "=STDEVA(C5:BE5)"}
